<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fertile Crescent</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as al-hilāl al-ḵhaṣīb</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Fertile Crescent is a crescent-shaped region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> spanning modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, Israel, Jordan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, together with northern Kuwait, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. It is believed to be the first region where settled farming emerged, and it was home to the eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> founder crops, among them emmer wheat, einkorn, barley and lentil, and to four of the five most important domesticated animals. Early civilisations such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> flourished there, and irrigation, writing, the wheel and glass first appeared in the region. The archaeologist James Henry Breasted popularised the term in 1914 and 1916.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 10,000 BCE Natufians carried parthenocarpic figs from Africa to the south-eastern corner of the <b>Fertile Crescent</b>, and they may have visited Cyprus, which would have meant crossing considerable stretches of open water.</blockquote><a href="/culture/natufians">Natufians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
